**The conflict scenario:**

```java
interface RBICompliant {
    default String getComplianceReport() {
        return "RBI Standard Compliance Report";
    }
}

interface SEBICompliant {
    default String getComplianceReport() {  // SAME name + signature!
        return "SEBI Standard Compliance Report";
    }
}

// COMPILE ERROR! "class TradingBank inherits unrelated defaults for getComplianceReport()"
// class TradingBank implements RBICompliant, SEBICompliant { }
```

**Fix 1 — Override with own implementation:**

```java
class TradingBank implements RBICompliant, SEBICompliant {
    @Override
    public String getComplianceReport() {
        return "TradingBank Custom Compliance Report";  // own logic
    }
}
```

**Fix 2 — Delegate to ONE interface using InterfaceName.super:**

```java
class TradingBank implements RBICompliant, SEBICompliant {
    @Override
    public String getComplianceReport() {
        return RBICompliant.super.getComplianceReport();  // delegates to RBI
    }
}
```

**Fix 3 — Combine BOTH:**

```java
class TradingBank implements RBICompliant, SEBICompliant {
    @Override
    public String getComplianceReport() {
        return RBICompliant.super.getComplianceReport()
            + " + " + SEBICompliant.super.getComplianceReport();
    }
}
```

**Resolution priority rules (three-tier):**

Rule 1 — CLASS WINS: If a class defines a method, it beats ALL interface defaults.

Rule 2 — SUB-INTERFACE WINS: If interface B extends interface A and both have the default, B's version wins because it is more specific.

Rule 3 — MUST OVERRIDE: If neither rule 1 nor rule 2 applies (two unrelated interfaces), the class MUST override.

```java
interface A { default String name() { return "A"; } }
interface B extends A { default String name() { return "B"; } }  // B is more specific

class C implements A, B { }
// NO error! B.name() wins because B extends A (sub-interface is more specific)
// c.name() → "B"
```

